 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "In 1963, one of the tallest dams in the world did its job perfectly. And two thousand people died anyway.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The Vajont Dam in the Italian Alps was a huge wall, 262 meters tall.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Behind it, a lake filled a narrow valley between two steep mountains.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But the slope on one side was weak rock, sitting on layers of slippery clay.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Every time engineers raised the water, the mountain crept a little more.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Water soaked the clay and made it even slicker.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "On October 9th, at night, the whole slope let go.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "About 270 million cubic meters of rock slid into the lake in under a minute.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It pushed the water up like a giant hand in a bathtub.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A wave taller than the dam itself rolled right over the top.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It crashed into the town of Longarone below, and wiped it off the map.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And the dam? It's still standing today, almost undamaged.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The concrete was fine. The problem was the mountain next to it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why engineers now study the hills around a reservoir as hard as the dam itself.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A dam is only as safe as the valley it sits in. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.2
  }
 ],
